# Day 07: DNS Administration with PowerShell

## Overview

On Day 07 of my PowerShell 10 Days: Zero to Hero challenge, I focused on DNS Administration using PowerShell on Windows Server 2022.

The goal of this practical session was to understand how PowerShell can help System Administrators manage DNS zones, troubleshoot DNS resolution, create DNS records, and perform DNS zone exports and backups.

## Key Learning Areas

- Advanced DNS Management
- DNS Troubleshooting
- DNS Record Management
- Creating DNS A Records
- DNS Zone Export
- DNS Backup Verification

## Hands-on Practical Exercises

### Lab 1 – Advanced DNS Management

Inspect DNS zones and DNS records using PowerShell.

```powershell
Get-DnsServerZone

Get-DnsServerResourceRecord -ZoneName "Abdirhman.local"
```

### Lab 2 – DNS Troubleshooting

Practice DNS troubleshooting and investigate DNS resolution and client cache.

```powershell
Resolve-DnsName microsoft.com

Get-DnsClientCache

Get-DnsServerForwarder
```

### Lab 3 – DNS Record Management

Create a test DNS A record in the Active Directory DNS zone.

```powershell
$ZoneName = (Get-ADDomain).DNSRoot

Add-DnsServerResourceRecordA `
-ZoneName $ZoneName `
-Name "lab-test" `
-IPv4Address "10.20.27.129"
```

Verify the DNS record:

```powershell
Get-DnsServerResourceRecord `
-ZoneName $ZoneName `
-Name "lab-test"
```

Test DNS name resolution:

```powershell
Resolve-DnsName lab-test.$ZoneName
```

### Lab 4 – DNS Server Backup

Export the DNS zone to a file.

```powershell
$ZoneName = (Get-ADDomain).DNSRoot

Export-DnsServerZone `
-Name $ZoneName `
-FileName "Abdirhman-backup.dns"
```

Copy the exported DNS zone file into the backup directory.

```powershell
Copy-Item `
"C:\Windows\System32\DNS\Abdirhman-backup.dns" `
"C:\DNS-Backup"
```

Verify the backup file:

```powershell
Get-ChildItem "C:\DNS-Backup" -Recurse |
Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime
```

